//! THE THREAD TABLE, THE FRAME ARENA AND THE READY QUEUE — a thread's STATE,
//! held below everything that acts on it.
//!
//! What is NOT here is the SWITCH (`sched`) and the exit (`sched` again,
//! because retiring the last thread ends the process): this module is the
//! storage and the queue discipline, so the seams that wake a thread — a
//! waiter's delivery, a timer's expiry — can push one onto the ready queue
//! without reaching for the scheduler that will pick it up.

import hal

import kcore.limits.{
    FRAME_ARENA_WORDS,
    MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS,
    MAX_THREADS,
    NO_PROCESS,
    NO_THREAD,
    THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE,
}
import kcore.mem.{align_up}
import kcore.slab.{Slab}

// =============================================================================
// Processes, threads and the scheduler (design 178 D2/D3/D4; spec §2, §7, §8)
// =============================================================================
//
// THE TRAP FRAME IS THE THREAD CONTEXT, and that one decision is most of this
// unit. A thread's saved registers are the frame the HAL's trap entry writes on
// the way in and reads on the way out; a thread SWITCH is therefore not a
// separate save/restore at all — it is `ktrap` returning a DIFFERENT frame
// address than it was called with. Entering user mode for the first time is the
// same operation with a frame nothing has run in yet. Two seam calls carry the
// whole of it: `hal.frame_init` builds a fresh context, `hal.resume_frame`
// enters one, and the trap entry's ordinary return path does every switch after
// that.
//
// WHERE A SWITCH CAN HAPPEN is design 178's D2, and the frame model is what
// makes it free: the only place a frame address is chosen is the user-return
// boundary, because that is the only place `ktrap` returns. The kernel is never
// preempted, holds no lock, and has no half-saved context to protect — the
// state a switch reads is a table, and the tick that motivates one only sets a
// flag.
//
// THE PROTECTION-DOMAIN RELOAD IS NOT HERE, and its absence is deliberate
// rather than forgotten (design 178 D4 + the M3 memory notes). Threads of one
// process share one domain, so a same-process switch reloads nothing; the
// reload belongs at exactly one point — `kcore.sched`'s `pick_next`, where the
// outgoing and incoming threads' PROCESSES are both in hand — and sawos design
// 2 D-5 installed it there and nowhere else. Whether that reload is a register
// file or a table root is the HAL's business, which is why this paragraph can
// say it without naming either. What DOES live here is the one word the
// scheduler compares against: `LAST_PROT_PROCESS`, below, beside the rest of
// the state a switch reads.

/// EXTENT 0's frames: every compiled thread's saved context, back to back.
///
/// All-zero, so it costs no image bytes (design 149) — and a zeroed frame is
/// also the right initial state, since `frame_init` only has to write the three
/// or four words that are not zero.
///
/// It stays a bare array rather than becoming part of `THREADS` (sawos design
/// 34), and `frame_slot` below says why: the alignment is load-bearing, and a
/// `Slab`'s inline array cannot promise it.
unsafe static var THREAD_FRAMES: [UInt64; FRAME_ARENA_WORDS] = [0; FRAME_ARENA_WORDS]

/// The address of thread slot `index`'s frame.
///
/// # THE CONTEXT-SWITCH PATH, AND WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE THIS
///
/// **THIS IS THE READ THE TRAP PATH MAKES WITH INTERRUPTS IMPLIED OFF.** Every
/// context switch in the system is `kcore.sched.run_thread` calling this and
/// `ktrap` returning the answer; the HAL's trap entry then resumes that frame.
/// M5 unit 6 (design 32) declined to convert THREADS for exactly this reason,
/// and unit 6a converts it under the rule that this function's hot arm must stay
/// the arithmetic it was.
///
/// **THE FLOOR ARM IS BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE OLD FUNCTION.** `index < MAX_THREADS` is
/// every thread on a machine that never donates — which is every case in the
/// suite — and that arm is the same `align_up` plus multiply-add it has always
/// been, now behind one compare against a compile-time constant. No walk, no
/// memory read of the extent table, no branch on donated state.
///
/// **THE DONATED ARM IS ONE BOUNDED WALK AND NO MORE.** `THREADS.locate` is at
/// most `MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS` (4) iterations of an O(1) body over the same table
/// the slot lookup uses, and the address is then a multiply-add on the side-arena
/// base recorded in that same row. AUDIT (design 1): no preemption point, and
/// none possible — the body allocates nothing, can fault on nothing (the extent
/// was validated and zeroed before it was installed, and donation is permanent
/// so a base is never retired), and takes no lock. The bound is finite because
/// extents are finitely many.
///
/// **THE ONE THING THAT COULD DISAGREE IS MADE UNABLE TO.** Slot `i`'s frame and
/// slot `i`'s storage come from ONE `locate` answer — the same extent, the same
/// offset — so a donated thread cannot read another thread's frame however the
/// chain was built. That is what `Slab.ext_side` is for, and it is why the frame
/// base is a COLUMN of the extent table rather than a parallel array.
///
/// # WHY THE FRAME IS NOT A FIELD OF `ThreadSlot`
///
/// The obvious conversion — fold the frame into the slot, let the plain chain
/// carry both — is UNSOUND, and the reason is three instructions deep in one
/// profile's trap entry: its user-return path loads the frame address INTO THE
/// STACK POINTER, so a frame IS that register and must carry its alignment.
/// That is what `THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE % 16 == 0` in `kcore.limits` has always
/// been about, and the static_assert beside it is the statement of record.
///
/// A fattened `ThreadSlot` would be `sizeof(ThreadSlot) + THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE`
/// bytes at `alignof(Int)`. On one profile that is 592, a multiple of 16, so it
/// would have passed; on the other it is 552, which is 8 mod 16, so every other
/// frame would have been misaligned — ON THE PROFILE THAT DOES NOT CHECK.
/// Saw has no alignment attribute to pin a struct with, and no padding makes a
/// struct a multiple of 16 on both profiles at once (the two headers are 40 and
/// 80 bytes, so the parity the padding must fix is opposite on each). So the
/// arena stays separately aligned, at the stride, exactly as it always was.
///
/// **THE SECOND FUNNEL, NAMED** (sawos designs 29 + 32). `Slab.extent_addr` is
/// the funnel for donated SLOTS; this is the funnel for donated FRAMES, and it
/// is the only other place in the kernel where a stored physical base becomes a
/// kernel-dereferenced address. `ext_side[e]` stays PHYSICAL — a base is
/// addresses-as-data — and the `hal.phys_to_virt` below is the moment of
/// dereference, which is design 29's whole discipline. Getting this wrong is a
/// hard fault on a translating profile and INVISIBLE on an MPU one, which is
/// precisely what 029's rebase note warned the next unit about.
public(package) func frame_slot(index: Int) unsafe -> UInt {
    let stride = THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE as UInt
    if index < MAX_THREADS {
        let base = align_up(((&var THREAD_FRAMES) as UnsafePointer<UInt64>) as UInt, stride)
        return base + (index as UInt) * stride
    }
    let (e, rest) = THREADS.locate(index)
    hal.phys_to_virt(THREADS.side_base(e) + (rest as UInt) * stride)
}

/// What a thread slot is doing (spec §8).
///
/// `Exited` is a state a slot STAYS IN rather than a transition to `Free`,
/// because a join arriving after the exit must still find the exit code. The
/// slot returns to the pool at process teardown; reaping it earlier needs a
/// handle-close op, which M2 does not have — and without close, no slot is ever
/// reused, which is also why handles carry no generation yet (§3's
/// stale-handle detection has nothing to detect until a slot can be reused).
public(package) enum ThreadState: UInt8 {
    case Free = 0,
    case Created = 1,
    case Runnable = 2,
    case Running = 3,
    case Blocked = 4,
    case Exited = 5,
}

/// One thread (spec §2 table row).
///
/// `link` is the ONE list pointer a thread needs, because a thread is on at
/// most one list at a time: the ready queue when it is runnable, or the joiner
/// list of the thread it is waiting for when it is blocked. Slot indices are
/// stored PLUS ONE so that zero means "no thread", which is what a zeroed
/// static already says.
///
/// **THERE IS NO `refs` FIELD HERE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A DECISION** (sawos
/// design 7 D-1). Every other countable kind gained one; a Thread did not,
/// because the JOIN/EXIT PROTOCOL already owns a thread slot's lifetime and it
/// owns it on terms a handle count cannot express. `ThreadState.Exited` is a
/// state a slot STAYS IN so that a join arriving after the exit still finds the
/// exit code — a reader that holds no handle and is not counted anywhere — and
/// the joiner list is a second such claim on the slot. Folding those into a
/// reference count is real design (what is a joiner worth? what does a
/// zero-reference `Exited` slot owe a join that has not arrived?) with no
/// current payoff, since nothing yet runs out of thread slots before its
/// process ends. So the slot comes back at the teardown exactly as it always
/// has, the QUOTA row for threads is charged and only ever credited there, and
/// this paragraph is the record that the question was asked and deferred.
public(package) struct ThreadSlot {
    /// `public(package)` because PARKING A THREAD HAPPENS OUTSIDE THIS MODULE
    /// since the split: `waitables` blocks one on a Waiter's list. The state
    /// machine is still described here and nowhere else.
    public(package) state: ThreadState,
    public(package) process: Int,
    // (`self_handle` WAS HERE and is GONE — sawos design 3 D-4. It cached the
    // word `ThreadSelf` handed back, which was a fact about the thread only
    // while a getter answered the same handle every ask. Mint-per-call makes a
    // thread's own handle a capability INSTANCE the caller owns and may release,
    // so there is no single word to cache and a cached one would go stale at the
    // first release. `ProcessSlot.self_handle` went with it.)
    public(package) exit_code: UInt,
    /// Head of the list of threads blocked joining THIS one (slot + 1).
    public(package) joiners: Int,
    /// Next slot on whichever list this thread is on (slot + 1).
    /// `public(package)`: the list a parked thread is on may be a Waiter's,
    /// which `waitables` owns.
    public(package) link: Int,
    /// Where a PARKED THREAD wants its answer written — a user address, ALREADY
    /// VALIDATED by `copy_out_check` at the call. Validating at the call rather
    /// than at the wake is what makes a bad buffer terminate the process that
    /// supplied it, while it is still the running one.
    /// `public(package)` because the wait side is what sets it, at the park.
    ///
    /// **TWO PARKS USE IT SINCE M4 UNIT 3.5** (sawos design 17) and the field is
    /// still one field, because a thread is inside at most one syscall: a
    /// `Waiter.Wait` writes a wait RECORD here, and a fused `PipeInlet.Call`
    /// writes the REPLY BODY here. Both are "the buffer this park will answer
    /// into", both were checked while the caller was running, and which one it
    /// is is said by the two fields below rather than by a second address.
    public(package) wait_buf: UInt,
    /// **THE FUSED CALL'S ROOM PARK: which connection this thread is waiting
    /// for space on** (slot + 1; 0 = it is not), sawos design 17.
    ///
    /// `PipeInletOp.Call` never answers `WouldBlock` — design 10 ruling 8's
    /// level, reached from inside the op — so a full ring parks the caller
    /// here, and the room the next settle makes is what resumes it. EXACTLY ONE
    /// of this field and `call_claim` is non-zero while a thread is inside a
    /// `Call`, and the pair names which of the two things it is waiting for.
    public(package) call_pipe: Int,
    /// **THE FUSED CALL'S REPLY PARK: which exchange this thread's answer is
    /// coming from** (exchange + 1; 0 = it is not waiting for one).
    ///
    /// **IT IS THE CLAIM ITSELF, NOT A NOTE ABOUT ONE.** A fused call mints no
    /// `PipeReply` handle (design 10 ruling 4's rider: zero handle traffic on
    /// the fast path), so the parked THREAD is what holds the exchange's reply
    /// column open — one reference, taken at the stage and dropped at the wake.
    /// `PIPE_CALLER` is the same fact indexed the other way, which is what lets
    /// a settling exchange find the thread without a scan.
    public(package) call_claim: Int,
    /// The request body's address in the caller's memory, and its length —
    /// live only while `call_pipe` is set.
    ///
    /// A ROOM-PARKED CALL HAS NOT STAGED ANYTHING YET, so the message is still
    /// where the caller put it and the resume has to copy it then. The address
    /// went through `copy_in_check` at the call, while the caller was running,
    /// which is what makes the later copy unable to fail.
    public(package) call_src: UInt,
    public(package) call_len: UInt,
}

/// **AN EXTENT CHAIN SINCE M5 UNIT 6a** (sawos design 34), with the frame arena
/// riding the extent table's side column — see `frame_slot`. `THREADS[i]` names
/// what it always named; the loop bounds below read `capacity()`.
public(package) unsafe static var THREADS: Slab<ThreadSlot, MAX_THREADS> = Slab<ThreadSlot, MAX_THREADS>(
    inline: [ThreadSlot(state: ThreadState.Free, process: 0,
                        exit_code: 0, joiners: 0, link: 0, wait_buf: 0,
                        call_pipe: 0, call_claim: 0, call_src: 0,
                        call_len: 0); MAX_THREADS],
    ext_base: [0; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    ext_slots: [0; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    ext_side: [0; MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS],
    ext_count: 0,
    total: MAX_THREADS)

/// The running thread's slot, or `NO_THREAD`.
///
/// It really is none before the first process exists, and it stays none for the
/// harness kernels that enter user mode without one — those cases predate
/// Thread objects and must go on working, which is what every `NO_THREAD` guard
/// below is for. Compound global state on design 149's terms: uniprocessor
/// kernel, interrupts taken from user mode only (D2), so the only writer is the
/// trap path and nothing can interleave with it.
public(package) unsafe static var CURRENT_THREAD: Int = NO_THREAD

/// The ready queue: a FIFO of runnable threads, head and tail as slot + 1.
/// The RUNNING thread is not on it — round-robin is "put the outgoing thread
/// at the tail, take the head", and both halves happen in `pick_next`.
public(package) unsafe static var READY_HEAD: Int = 0

public(package) unsafe static var READY_TAIL: Int = 0

/// Set by the tick when a thread's slice runs out, and by `Yield`. Read and
/// cleared at the user-return boundary, which is the only place it can act.
public(package) unsafe static var NEED_RESCHED: Bool = false

/// Ticks left in the running thread's slice.
public(package) unsafe static var SLICE_LEFT: UInt = 0

/// Ticks a thread runs before the timer takes the processor away (spec §7's
/// fixed timeslice). ONE tick, because M2's tick period is already the unit the
/// scheduler cares about and a longer slice would only make the preemption
/// harness wait for it.
public(package) static SLICE_TICKS: UInt = 1

/// Whose protection domain is CURRENTLY INSTALLED in the hardware, or
/// `NO_PROCESS` when none is (sawos design 2 D-5).
///
/// THE ONE WORD THAT MAKES THE RELOAD CHEAP. A switch between two threads of one
/// process must reload nothing — that is every switch every M2 case makes, and
/// keeping it free is what keeps the shipped transcripts untouched — so the
/// scheduler compares the incoming thread's process against this and does the
/// work only when they differ. It is set by whoever installs a domain, which is
/// the boot path (through `start_process`) and `kcore.sched`'s reload, and
/// INVALIDATED when a process is torn down, so a slot reused by a later child
/// can never inherit the dead one's map by number.
///
/// It lives here rather than beside the reload because it is scheduler state in
/// exactly the sense `CURRENT_THREAD` is, and because `process` — which is below
/// the scheduler — has to write it at boot.
public(package) unsafe static var LAST_PROT_PROCESS: Int = NO_PROCESS

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The ready queue
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

public(package) func ready_push(slot: Int) unsafe {
    THREADS[slot].link = 0
    if READY_TAIL == 0 {
        READY_HEAD = slot + 1
        READY_TAIL = slot + 1
    } else {
        THREADS[READY_TAIL - 1].link = slot + 1
        READY_TAIL = slot + 1
    }
}

public(package) func ready_pop() unsafe -> Int {
    if READY_HEAD == 0 {
        return NO_THREAD
    }
    let slot = READY_HEAD - 1
    READY_HEAD = THREADS[slot].link
    if READY_HEAD == 0 {
        READY_TAIL = 0
    }
    THREADS[slot].link = 0
    slot
}

/// Take every thread of process `p` OFF the ready queue, leaving everybody
/// else's where they were (sawos design 2 D-6).
///
/// THIS IS WHAT REPLACED `READY_HEAD = READY_TAIL = 0` in the teardown, and the
/// zeroing was not a shortcut so much as a ONE-PROCESS SPELLING: with a single
/// process every runnable thread belonged to the process that was ending, so
/// emptying the queue and removing its threads were the same act. With two they
/// are not, and the wholesale version would take ROOT's runnable threads away
/// when a child died.
///
/// A singly-linked walk with a trailing pointer, because that is what the list
/// is — `link` is the one list pointer a thread has (see `ThreadSlot`), so
/// there is no back pointer to unlink through and no second list to keep in
/// step. `READY_TAIL` follows the removal rather than being recomputed: it is
/// the only other word the queue has, and it is wrong exactly when the removed
/// node was the last one.
///
/// AUDIT (design 1): NO PREEMPTION POINT. As many iterations as the thread slab
/// is long at most (`MAX_THREADS` plus whatever a deployment donated — sawos
/// design 34; finite because extents are finitely many and each is finitely
/// sized), of an O(1) body — and the ready queue IS state the IRQ path writes
/// (`ready_push` from a wake), so a delivery landing mid-walk is precisely the
/// in-flight invariant the placement rule refuses.
public(package) func ready_remove_process(p: Int) unsafe {
    // Slot + 1 throughout, matching the list's own encoding: 0 means "no node".
    var prev: Int = 0
    var cur = READY_HEAD
    while cur != 0 {
        let slot = cur - 1
        let next = THREADS[slot].link
        if THREADS[slot].process == p {
            if prev == 0 {
                READY_HEAD = next
            } else {
                THREADS[prev - 1].link = next
            }
            if READY_TAIL == cur {
                READY_TAIL = prev
            }
            THREADS[slot].link = 0
        } else {
            prev = cur
        }
        cur = next
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Allocation
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Take a free thread slot, or `NO_THREAD` when the slab is empty.
public(package) func alloc_thread() unsafe -> Int {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < THREADS.capacity() {
        match THREADS[i].state {
            case Free -> { return i },
            case _ -> {},
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    NO_THREAD
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Thread lifecycle
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// How many of a process's threads have not exited.
public(package) func live_threads(p: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    var live: Int = 0
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < THREADS.capacity() {
        if THREADS[i].process == p {
            match THREADS[i].state {
                case Free -> {},
                case Exited -> {},
                case _ -> { live = live + 1 },
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    live
}

/// Park the running thread on `target`'s joiner list.
public(package) func block_on_join(slot: Int, target: Int) unsafe {
    THREADS[slot].state = ThreadState.Blocked
    THREADS[slot].link = THREADS[target].joiners
    THREADS[target].joiners = slot + 1
    NEED_RESCHED = true
}

/// The calling process. In M2 that is the process of the running thread, and
/// before there are threads it is the only process there is — the harness
/// kernels that enter user mode with no object model still make syscalls.
public(package) func calling_process() unsafe -> Int {
    if CURRENT_THREAD == NO_THREAD {
        return 0
    }
    THREADS[CURRENT_THREAD].process
}
